# vNext Skill: execute-step

## Default runtime role

runtime_role: assistance/v1

Follow `.workflow-system/WORKFLOW_PROTOCOL.md` and the management API in
`.workflow-system/runtime/support/ASSISTANCE_API.md`. Use the native service:
`node .workflow-system/runtime/support/assistance.mjs <command> --root <project>`.
Source-repository equivalent: `node runtime/vnext/support/assistance.mjs`.

Use `task-status` (or the `context.management` view) to identify the task, adopted
plan, step, review/disposition and actual commits. It reads the complete journal,
not one search page or the old CURRENT_TASK tuple. `find`/`read` remain evidence tools.
For task lifecycle changes use `task` actions documented in ASSISTANCE_API; raw
`record` is for general observations, not a substitute for task registration.
Normal management updates are part of the requested task operation, not optional
future reconciliation. Check `recorded`, `association` and `projection` separately.
If an association/view update fails, retain facts, try a bounded deterministic rebuild
when appropriate, and present concrete link/correct/resolve/defer choices. Honor the
chosen recovery without repeating business actions or asking again for existing consent.
A read-only request uses live task-status without writing a cache. Never declare
full task-management completion merely because a generic record was saved.

If a management operation fails, preserve its actual error and any output already
saved; use another authorized recording method when available and continue
independent authorized work. Do not retry unchanged calls, rerun business commands,
invent IDs/PASS or claim persistence succeeded. Ask only about a genuinely unresolved
user choice or unavailable business prerequisite, not an internal gate code.

## Workflow deviations

Apply **One-time confirmation for workflow deviations** in WORKFLOW_PROTOCOL for
all materially affected actions, not only approvals. Combine known consequences,
reuse an applicable explicit decision, then execute the user's chosen action without
legacy admission or repeated confirmation. Pure management errors need no consent;
report real gaps without making them PASS. Pause only an unresolved user choice.

## Method

Read the fresh task-status and select the requested mode before checking for new
code work. `default` implements the current step; `repair` addresses reviewed
findings; `finish` disposes the current step after checking its actual execution,
applicable review and remaining gaps. A bare invocation does not infer `finish`
from the absence of code changes. If the step is already executed and reviewed,
report `next_route: execute-step` with `next_mode: finish`; do not copy an earlier
review's `close-task` suggestion while the step remains open. An explicit read-only
request remains read-only. In `finish` mode, do not repeat implementation or tests
solely to perform the step disposition.

If the step needs an independently scoped prerequisite, follow the protocol's Derived
prerequisite tasks section: preserve the unfinished step, record the scope handoff and
origin, and switch only under the actual choice. Do not finish it to manufacture a switch.
After a return, read continuation, waiting dependencies and original execution/review facts.
continue-step means resume the remaining work, not finish or reuse old evidence as current
proof. Honor the parent's retained findings and checks; reassess evidence affected by the
child changes. No automatic business execution solely because focus was restored.

Establish the current requested work and actual scope from the user's instruction,
existing plan and source. Runtime state is context, not permission. Read relevant
requirements, contracts, past findings and tests. An optional snapshot can preserve
before-state; failure to obtain it does not require preflight recovery to edit code.

Implement the authorized work and necessary local corrections. Do not expand into
unrelated work, touch excluded paths, fabricate user authorization or infer writes
from discovery alone. Distinguish a real workflow/scope choice from internal map,
quota, receipt or revision drift; apply the shared confirmation rule to the former,
not the latter.

Run only the authorized, necessary validation. Keep actual selectors, commands, outputs,
source versions, failures and not-run checks. Do not add persistent tests or full-suite/E2E
runs merely to satisfy a workflow record. Use `task` action `execution` and/or `test`,
retaining actual task_id, plan_ref, step_id and source_revision; raw reports may be
attached/referenced separately, even when results fail, plans changed or links cannot be
resolved. Never change failed to passed to satisfy a completion condition.

Implementation self-check is normal work, not a formal review. By default do not
create a new formal review, including via record-review-result. An existing explicit
instruction covering execution AND review may be completed in this interaction;
follow review-change methods and call it self-review when the implementer reviews.
Do not claim independent review. Existing real reviews may be referenced without
manufacturing a new one. Record the actual outcome and remaining work; no mandatory
complete-executed-step or complete-reviewed-step call is needed to continue.

In `finish` mode, inspect the latest applicable review, checks and open
findings, not just a legacy completed/clean flag. If a workflow commitment is unmet,
apply the shared rule before that action. For example: "No applicable review pass was
found. Finish this step while keeping review incomplete, or review first?" After the
user chooses to finish, or when no material deviation needs a new choice, use
`task` action `step` with state `finished` (or the chosen disposition),
actual task/plan/step, decision link and remaining work; do not manufacture a clean review or call an
old completion gate to obtain permission. Finishing alone does not authorize the next
step or task closure. Changed plans, scope, validation and ordering use the same rule;
necessary in-scope implementation details and management drift do not trigger it.

In `finish` mode, verify the review still covers the actual work; a Git commit
alone does not change the reviewed files. Record the current task/plan/step and
review reference, then read task-status again. If the disposition was not saved,
report that fact and do not present the step as finished.

Read task-status after recording: execution, review and step disposition are separate.
Keep historical/late results marked historical; they must not reopen closed work or
replace the current execution. Report an unassociated result and link/correct it,
not repeat the actual execution. The view suggests the next unfinished step without
automatically executing it.
Base `next_route` on this fresh view after any step disposition; an earlier review's
route cannot override the current step state. After successfully finishing a
nonfinal step, if Git commits are within the user's task scope and there are
actual in-scope changes to save, recommend `next_route: git-commit` in this
execute-step result. State the exact `commit_scope`, excluding unrelated changes.
This is a one-time recommendation at the finish boundary, not a task-status stage,
permission to commit, or a prerequisite for starting the next step. The fresh
task-status can already point to the next unfinished step. If this was the last
step, recommend `next_route: close-task`; a final Git commit may follow closure
when requested. If Git is excluded or no relevant changes remain, follow the
fresh task-status route. Never present a failed or unrecorded finish as finished.

## Result

When enabled product maintenance and existing authorization cover the related
scope, use maintain-project references/reconcile.md to incorporate actual new
execution/test sources into the selected delivery assessment or pending_sources.
Read actual scope and subjects; unchanged requirement hash and task disposition
are not verification. Report document/task-operation results separately. Deferred
or failed maintenance does not prevent authorized implementation, Git, step
disposition, closure or stopping. Read-only requests only return pending locators.

Return the actual requested result, affected scope, evidence/record references,
remaining work and any unsaved/unreconciled data. Preserve existing user decisions
and exclusions; an explicit read-only request remains read-only. A record's successful
write does not certify the product or expand authorization.

Always include `next_route` in the final result: one public Skill ID or `null`,
with a brief reason. Choose it from the actual outcome and remaining work; use
`next_route: null` when no public Skill is justified. Finish any further stages
already covered by the user's instruction before choosing the route. The route is
advice, not permission or the only allowed next action. Do not invoke it solely
because it was recommended or widen validation or external effects without authorization.

